{:edb/db-before-t 162 :edb/db-after-t 163 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533475 50 #inst "2026-10-11T01:22:42.527Z" 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186054757 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398644 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186064211 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398875 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186064211 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398882 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186064380 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398861 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186078410 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398854 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186087773 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398686 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186087773 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398693 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186092334 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398833 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186101003 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398651 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186105717 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398868 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186115242 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398706 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186115737 1068 #edb/ref 17592186113358 13194139533475 17592186045585 false] [17592186115737 1068 #edb/ref 17592186398700 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186115737 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186115737 1071 "The inverse of the matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to each step?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186115737 1071 "The inverse of the matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to each step?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}A & I\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\left.2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right|___________; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\left.2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\right|___________; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533475 17592186045585 false] [17592186115737 1074 "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${A}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186120248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117617 13194139533475 17592186045585 false] [17592186120248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186398746 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186120248 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186120248 1071 "The equations of a curve $r(t)$ and its corresponding unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at an arbitrary point are given below. Find the principal normal vector to that curve at the point $P(0, 3, 4)$.\n$r(t)|= 5\\cos ti + 3\\sin tj + 4\\sin tk, t ∈ [0, 2π); T(t)|=-\\sin ti + \\frac{3}{5}\\cos tj + \\frac{4}{5}\\cos tk, t ∈ [0, 2π)$" 13194139533475 17592186045585 false] [17592186120248 1071 "The equations of a curve $r(t)$ and its corresponding unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at an arbitrary point are given below. Find the principal normal vector to that curve at the point $P(0,3,4).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ) \\\\ T(t) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186120248 1074 "First, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(0,3,4)$ on the given curve. We know that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨0,3,4⟩ \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+3\\text{ }j+4\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components of $i$, $j$, and $k$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=3 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(0,3,4)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖ & =\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+{(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}+\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(0,3,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖}=\\frac{-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k}{1}=-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =(-i)\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(i\\cdot j)+\\frac{4}{5}(i\\cdot k) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186124522 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398820 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186124591 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398630 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186124685 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398665 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186124685 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398672 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186124686 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398658 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186124686 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398679 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186133961 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398847 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186133963 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398840 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186134254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131533 13194139533475 17592186045585 false] [17592186134254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186398814 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186134254 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186134254 1071 "Find a basis of $H^{⊥}$ if $H = Span{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}$." 13194139533475 17592186045585 false] [17592186134254 1071 "Find a basis of ${H}^{⊥}$ if $H=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}.$" 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186134254 1074 "Let's denote the vector that spans the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}.$ \n\nA vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which is already in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one), so ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables. From the equation, we get ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2}-\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{3}.$ \nSo, every vector in ${H}^{⊥}$ has the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2}-\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${H}^{⊥}.$" 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186134324 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131953 13194139533475 17592186045585 false] [17592186134324 1068 #edb/ref 17592186398827 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186134324 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186134324 1071 "$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}],\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {v}_{1} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]\\,,[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]\\,,[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nSuppose that $V=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$ Given that $B$ is an orthogonal basis for $V,$ and that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186134324 1071 "$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, a_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix}, B = {[ \\mid; v_{1}; \\mid ], [ \\mid; v_{2}; \\mid ], \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}}$\n\nSuppose that $V = Span{a_{1}, a_{2}, a_{3}}$. Given that $B$ is an orthogonal basis for $V$, and that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}$?" 13194139533475 17592186045585 false] [17592186134324 1074 "We find an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1.** We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2.** We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{10}{5}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We find the vector ${v}_{3}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{15}{5}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{46}{46}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=1,$ $b=-2,$ and $\\frac{a}{b}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186147511 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398739 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186152058 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398637 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186152296 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398732 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186152568 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149815 13194139533475 17592186045585 false] [17592186152568 1068 #edb/ref 17592186398726 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186152568 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186152568 1071 "![](images/bc/bcf4076849dd96858c586f8696414789b3f68074b87652601245407142f2b8fc.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t)$, where $u$ denotes the unit step function?" 13194139533475 17592186045585 false] [17592186152568 1071 "![](images/bc/bcf4076849dd96858c586f8696414789b3f68074b87652601245407142f2b8fc.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t),$ where $u$ denotes the unit step function?" 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186152568 1074 "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/9f/9fb8c20672705e9d68e69d32e7ff3499ec71560e10aa85136b3f3ad75c5fb5ed.png)\n\nNotice that to transform $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we scale $y=u(t)$ by a factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, giving $y=2u(t).$\n\n- There is no horizontal shift or offset.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=2u(t).$" 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186157452 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398719 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186166909 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398752 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186166909 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398759 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186166909 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398766 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186166909 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398773 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186166979 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398800 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186166979 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398807 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186167172 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164039 13194139533475 17592186045585 false] [17592186167172 1068 #edb/ref 17592186398713 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186167172 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186167172 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2xy,\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the modified Euler method. What entry should be placed in the cell indicated in the table below, if the step size is $Δx=1?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $2$ | $\\,?\\,$ |  |  |  |  |  |" 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186167172 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = 2xy, y(1) = 1$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the modified Euler method. What entry should be placed in the cell indicated in the table below, if the step size is $Δx = 1$?\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $Δy_{p}$ | $y_{p}$ | $y_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $2$ | $$? |  |  |  |  |  |" 13194139533475 17592186045585 false] [17592186167172 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe Modified Euler Method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2xy \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ |  |  |  |\n\nThe coordinates of the initially predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{\\text{pred}} & =y+Δ{y}_{\\text{pred}}=1+2=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\nSo, the predicted slope at this point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =2\\cdot {x}_{\\text{new}}\\cdot {y}_{p} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $12$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2+12)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 14 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $12$ | $7$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy=1+7=8.\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $12$ | $7$ |\n| $2$ | $8$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈8.$" 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186198464 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398617 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186198728 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194964 13194139533475 17592186045585 false] [17592186198728 1068 #edb/ref 17592186398624 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186198728 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186198728 1071 "$\\,$ $x\\in R$\n\nThe predicate $P(x)$ is given above. Which of following represents $¬P(x)?$" 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186198728 1071 "$x ∈ R$\n\nThe predicate $P(x)$ is given above. Which of following represents $¬P(x)$?" 13194139533475 17592186045585 false] [17592186198728 1074 "In plain English, our predicate $P(x)$ means the following: \n\n$\\,$ $x$ is a member of the set of real numbers.\n\nThe symbol \"$¬$\" represents negation. To negate our plain English expression, we place the words \"It is not true that\" in front of it. This gives the following:\n\n$\\,$ It is not true that $x$ is a member of the set of real numbers.\n\nIn other words, $x$ is *not* a member of $R.$ Therefore, $¬P(x)$ means the following:\n\n$\\,$ $x\\notin R$" 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186221426 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398787 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186221467 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398780 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186222005 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220672 13194139533475 17592186045585 false] [17592186222005 1068 #edb/ref 17592186398794 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186222005 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186222005 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]x,\\,{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ shown above. Which of the following is a fundamental matrix for the system?" 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186222005 1071 "$x^{′} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x, x_{1}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos 2t \\\\ \\sin 2t\\end{bmatrix}e^{t}, x_{2}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\sin 2t \\\\ \\cos 2t\\end{bmatrix}e^{t}$\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$, shown above. Which of the following is a fundamental matrix for the system?" 13194139533475 17592186045585 false] [17592186222005 1074 "Given a linear system ${x}^{′}=Ax,$ where $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $Φ(t)$ of the system is an $n\\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our case, we have a $2\\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t \\\\ {e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}=[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t \\\\ {e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our fundamental could be written as\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & -{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t \\\\ {e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t & {e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $Φ$ can be written in any order." 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186398617 1068 #edb/ref 17592186398618 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186398617 1069 "q-175312" 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186398617 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186398617 1071 "$\\,$ $x=3$\n\nThe predicate $P(x)$ is given above. Which of following represents $¬P(x)?$" 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186398617 1072 #uuid "a6022d77-1162-5219-998d-09e9a6dcc354" 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186398617 1074 "In plain English, our predicate $P(x)$ means the following:\n\n$\\,$ $x$ equals $3.$\n\nThe symbol \"$¬$\" represents negation. To negate our plain English expression, we place the words \"It is not true that\" in front of it. This gives the following:\n\n$\\,$ It is not true that $x$ equals $3.$\n\nIn other words, $x$ is *not equal to* $3.$ Therefore, $¬P(x)$ means the following:\n\n$\\,$ $x\\ne 3$" 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186398618 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398619 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186398618 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398620 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186398618 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398621 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186398618 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398622 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186398618 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398623 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186398618 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186398618 1077 "selection" 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186398618 1079 #edb/ref 17592186398623 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186398618 1080 #uuid "73823890-a9ed-5615-9941-0b6a5d3d5d74" 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186398619 1081 "x=-3" 131941395334