6045585 true] [17592186398840 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186398840 1071 "Suppose that the function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+1, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 5-2x, & x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function?" 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186398840 1072 #uuid "4f66078a-9fc7-5c7e-ae29-7151d1232eff" 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186398840 1074 "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at $x=1.$ To see this, we note that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=2\\ne 3={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the point $x=1.$ \n\nFrom the given options, only the interval $[-2,0]$ is allowed.  The intervals $(1,3]$ and $[-2,1)$ are not closed, $[-2,1]$ and $[0,3]$ contain the point $x=1.$" 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186398841 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398842 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186398841 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398843 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186398841 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398844 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186398841 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398845 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186398841 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398846 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186398841 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186398841 1077 "selection" 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186398841 1079 #edb/ref 17592186398842 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186398841 1080 #uuid "b49fc3fc-44cb-5db2-9fc5-ed9e471c021f" 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186398842 1081 "[-2,0]" 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592186398842 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533475 17592186045585 true] [17592